# The orbit-count function determines the number of fixed points

For a finite group `G` acting on a finite set `X` and an element `σ : G`, write

* `orbitCount σ X` for the number of orbits of the cyclic group `⟨σ⟩` on `X`;
* `fixedCount σ X` for the number of points of `X` fixed by `σ`.

The main result of this file is `NK.WeakNU.fixedCount_eq_of_orbitCount_eq`: if two finite
`G`-sets `X` and `Y` have the same number of `⟨σ ^ i⟩`-orbits for every `i`, then they have the
same number of `σ`-fixed points.  This is the combinatorial input of the Perlis/Gassmann step of
the weak Neukirch–Uchida theorem.

The proof is Burnside's lemma together with the structure of the subgroups of a cyclic group.
Writing `n = orderOf σ`, for every `m ∣ n` Burnside's lemma applied to the (cyclic) subgroup
`⟨σ ^ (n / m)⟩` of order `m` gives

`m * orbitCount (σ ^ (n / m)) X = ∑ g ∈ ⟨σ ^ (n / m)⟩, #X ^ g`,

and grouping the elements of `⟨σ ^ (n / m)⟩` by their order (there are exactly `Nat.totient d`
elements of order `d`, all of which generate the same subgroup `⟨σ ^ (n / d)⟩`) turns the right
hand side into `∑ d ∈ m.divisors, Nat.totient d * fixedCount (σ ^ (n / d)) X`.  Comparing this
relation for `X` and `Y` and inducting on `m` in the divisor order yields the theorem.
-/
import Mathlib
import Submission.NK.WeakNU.Defs
